On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 4:46 PM, .arpis. <rpglover64+jbobau@gmail.com>wrote:

> Right. I'm not criticizing the definition, merely explicitly pointing out
> notable differences from others and linguistic choices made.
>
> As for philosophy, someone else said it better than I can:
>
> > Philosophy has some amount of a problem in that whenever something
> becomes rigorous enough we often stop classifying it as philosophy (math,
> physics, linguistics, economics, psychology- pretty much all of these split
> off from philosophy).
>   -- http://www.scottaaronson.com/blog/?p=735#comment-26093
>

Yeah, basically all of the scientific disciplines- and the discipline of
Science itself, derived from philosophy. It is my hope that at some point
philosophy will cease to be simply because everything becomes explainable
and knowable.


> On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 6:38 PM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>> On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 4:31 PM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com>wrote:
>>
>>> On Mon, Dec 31, 2012 at 3:52 PM, John E Clifford <kali9putra@yahoo.com>wrote:
>>>
>>>> Sorry, to intrude philosophy (or theology) into this, but a case can be
>>>> made that the only actual god has no worshipers, since all have worshiped
>>>> imperfect simulcra of it -- and always will, even though they keep trying
>>>> to get it right.  It is hard to worhsip the ineffable without effing a bit
>>>> -- and thus missing.
>>>>
>>>
>>> And that is precisely why I don't have truck with philosophy. I see
>>> absolutely no point in discussing things which can not be proven one way or
>>> the other. God is unknowable in every aspect, number, gender, temperament,
>>> existence. There is no way for us to find out anything about the true
>>> nature of (the) god(s), and so there's no point that I can see in bothering
>>> with trying. And like pretty much everything ever thought of by
>>> philosophers, if and when we do figure out a way to make such things
>>> knowable, we'll probably find out that all the philosophers were wrong.
>>>
>>> Also, "the only actual god" sounds very Judeo-Christian. How could
>>> possibly know whether lo cevni is singular? Maybe there's three, or 20, or
>>> a million. The only thing we can say we know for certain is that we don't
>>> know anything.
>>>
>>
>> Sorry, I forgot to state my point.
>>
>> When I speak of gods, I don't bother about what is actual, specifically
>> because there's literally no what to know what that is. I have my own
>> beliefs about the matter, of course, but I don't pretend to think that my
>> beliefs are the definite truth. So, when I talk about gods, I talk about
>> beings like Thor, Ra, Gaeia, Minerva, and Pluto; beings which more than
>> likely do not now and never did exist, but which at some point in time-
>> whether or not "now" is part of that time frame- were believed to be the
>> real thing by somebody.
>>
>> So, my point is, I feel pretty safe in my assumption that the vast
>> majority about lo cevni will be about those which are believed in, and not
>> the ineffable actuality, and have no problem with forcing those who do wish
>> to talk about the ineffable actuality in Lojban to use lo cevni be zi'o to
>> do so.
>>
>>
>>
